Review Point Description:
[Scope] This rule reviews confidentiality provisions in contracts. The Protected Party means the party served by the rule whose interests are to be protected; the Counterparty means the other party to the contract.

[General Preliminary Rule: Determining the Obligated Party] Before assessing any control condition, the actual party bearing the relevant confidentiality obligation, confidentiality period, or liability for liquidated damages must first be identified:
- If the obligated party is the Counterparty (the provision binds only the Counterparty or imposes confidentiality obligations or liability for breach only on the Counterparty), never flag a risk and do not proceed to any subsequent control-condition assessment;
- If the provision confers rights on the Protected Party or is generally favorable to the Protected Party (for example, the Protected Party is the disclosing party or rights holder, and high liquidated damages are intended to bind the Counterparty), never flag a risk;
- Only where the Protected Party bears the relevant obligation may the assessment proceed to the applicable control conditions. The conclusion must identify the obligated party and the provision on which that determination is based.

[Definitions]
- Ancillary Document: An order, settlement statement, execution order, confirmation, amendment agreement, supplemental agreement, amendment, price memorandum, or similar document whose text cites the name or number of the master agreement (framework agreement), or provides that "matters not covered herein shall be governed by the master agreement/framework agreement" or "all other provisions of the original agreement shall remain unchanged." The confidentiality arrangements of such a document are deemed to be covered by the master agreement.
- Circumstances terminating confidentiality obligations: Any provision that extinguishes or renders inapplicable a confidentiality obligation, including "the information has entered the public domain/become publicly known/become publicly available," "disclosure with the disclosing party's written consent," "compelled disclosure under laws, regulations, or a regulatory authority," and "disclosure necessary to assert rights." Circumstances listed under headings such as "confidentiality exceptions," "circumstances of non-application," or "exclusions" have the same effect as "circumstances of termination."
- Loss-indemnity liquidated damages: Liquidated damages accompanied by language such as "if insufficient to cover the non-breaching party's losses, the breaching party shall continue to compensate the shortfall," "whichever of the liquidated damages and the actual losses is higher," or "limited to the actual losses." These are compensatory in nature and do not constitute fixed, high punitive liquidated damages.
- Reciprocal confidentiality arrangements: The Protected Party and the Counterparty have identical or substantially equivalent arrangements across all three dimensions: scope of confidential information, confidentiality period, and liquidated damages/compensation standard.

[Step 1: Identify whether a confidentiality provision exists]
Scan the entire contract to determine whether it contains a dedicated confidentiality provision or chapter, a standalone confidentiality agreement as an annex, or substantive confidentiality content in another provision (such as rights and obligations or liability for breach), including any one of the following: scope of confidential information, restrictions on use, restrictions on disclosure, confidentiality period, or liability for breach.
- If any of the foregoing substantive confidentiality arrangements exists: Treat this as "confidentiality provision identified" and proceed to Step 3.
- If none exists: Treat this as "no confidentiality provision identified" and proceed to Step 2.

[Step 2: Assessment where no confidentiality provision is identified]
Determine whether the contract falls within either of the following circumstances in which a confidentiality provision is mandatory:
(1) The contract name, title, or nature falls within any of the following: confidentiality agreement, NDA, technology development contract, technology transfer contract, technology licensing contract, technology consulting contract, or technical services contract;
(2) The subject matter, performance content, or annexes expressly involve any of the following specific categories of information (the information must actually be mentioned in the text; no inference may be made merely from the contract type):
    a. Non-public business information: customer lists, supplier lists, pricing systems, cost data, sales data, channel information, or bidding proposals;
    b. Non-public technical information: source code, design drawings, process flows, formulas, algorithms, models, test data, technical solutions, or research results;
    c. Non-public major decision-making information: mergers and acquisitions, reorganizations, investment and financing, listings, major personnel matters, and other business decisions;
    d. Processing of personal information: sensitive personal information of natural persons, such as identity document numbers, biometric information, financial accounts, medical or health information, and location or movement tracks, or batch processing of personal information involving more than 1,000 individuals.
[Control Condition] If either (1) or (2) applies and none of the following exemptions applies, flag a risk.
[Exemptions] No risk is flagged if any of the following applies:
- Neither (1) nor (2) applies;
- The contract is an Ancillary Document as defined in [Definitions], and its confidentiality arrangements are covered by the master agreement; the absence of a restated confidentiality provision in this document does not constitute a deficiency;
- None of the information categories in Step 2(2) appears by its specific name in the contract text, and the contract contains only general business terms such as "customer leads," "customer-retained information," "business data," or "operating information," or the information can only be inferred from the contract type or business background;
- The contract substantively protects the relevant information through dedicated provisions on data security, personal information protection, or restrictions on the use of intellectual property, such as least-privilege access, encryption or hashing, prohibition on retention, purpose limitation, or data deletion obligations, and is therefore deemed to provide substantive confidentiality protection.

[Step 3: Assessment where a confidentiality provision is identified]
[Control Conditions] Subject to [General Preliminary Rule: Determining the Obligated Party], flag a risk if any of the following applies:
(1) The contract name or nature is a confidentiality agreement/NDA, but any of the following substantive elements is missing: scope of confidential information, confidentiality period, or liability for breach;
(2) Both parties owe confidentiality obligations, but the Protected Party's obligations are more onerous than the Counterparty's in any of the following dimensions: a broader scope of confidential information, a longer confidentiality period, or a higher liquidated damages/compensation standard;
(3) The Protected Party alone owes confidentiality obligations, and the confidentiality period is stated as "perpetual," "long-term," or "indefinite," or no period is specified, while none of the following circumstances or methods for terminating the confidentiality obligation is provided: the information enters the public domain, disclosure with the disclosing party's written consent, or disclosure compelled by laws or regulations;
(4) Where the Protected Party (alone or as one of both parties) owes confidentiality obligations, either of the following thresholds is met by the cap on the total liquidated damages or compensation payable by the Protected Party for breach of those obligations: the cap exceeds 30% of the total contract value; or the amount is RMB 200,000 or more;
(5) The Counterparty alone owes confidentiality obligations, and the confidentiality period satisfies either of the following: it is shorter than the cooperation term/service term agreed in the contract; or its absolute duration is less than three years.
[Exemptions] No risk is flagged if any of the following applies:
(a) In Step 3(3), although perpetual confidentiality is agreed (including "long-term validity," "continuing validity," or "indefinite term"), any of the circumstances terminating confidentiality obligations under [Definitions] is expressly listed; equivalent provisions under headings such as "confidentiality exceptions," "circumstances of non-application," or "exclusions" are equally valid. Where the confidential subject matter is qualified by wording such as "non-public" or "not publicly disclosed," the boundary that the obligation terminates once the information enters the public domain is deemed to be included.
(b) In Step 3(4), the liquidated damages are loss-indemnity liquidated damages as defined, and therefore do not trigger a risk.
(c) In Step 3(2), the parties have reciprocal confidentiality arrangements as defined, so this does not constitute "the Protected Party's obligations being more onerous than the Counterparty's"; no risk arises even if the amount of liquidated damages exceeds the threshold.
(d) Limitation on applicability: Step 3(3) applies only where "the Protected Party alone owes confidentiality obligations" and does not apply where both parties owe confidentiality obligations. Step 3(5) applies only where "the Counterparty alone owes confidentiality obligations" and must not be applied in reverse to the Protected Party's obligations.
(e) In Step 3(4), where liquidated damages are calculated "per breach/per individual breach" and the amount for an individual breach does not reach the threshold, an excess must not be inferred by aggregating the consequences of multiple breaches.
(f) In Step 3(5), no risk is triggered where the confidentiality period is equal to the cooperation term/service term ("equal to" is not "shorter than"), or where the confidentiality obligation is a statutory obligation arising under laws or regulations (such as the Personal Information Protection Law or the Lawyers Law and professional rules) and no specific number of years is stated.

[Scope] This rule reviews the completeness and compliance of governing-law provisions in contracts. The Protected Party means the party served by the rule whose interests are to be protected; the Counterparty means the other party to the contract.

[Definitions]
- Governing-law provision: A provision that expressly specifies the governing laws and regulations used to resolve disputes related to the contract or interpret its provisions.
- Foreign-related contract: A contract with any of the following characteristics:
  a. A foreign-related party: One or more parties is a foreign individual, stateless person, foreign enterprise, or foreign organization;
  b. A foreign-related subject matter: Property, goods, or services involved in the contract are located outside the People's Republic of China;
  c. A foreign-related legal fact: The formation, performance, amendment, or termination of the contract occurs outside the People's Republic of China;
  d. Foreign-related applicable law: The contract provides for the application of foreign law or an international treaty.
- Overseas Protected Party entity: The Protected Party itself is an entity incorporated outside mainland China, including a company incorporated overseas or an entity incorporated in Hong Kong, Macao, or Taiwan, China.
- Reasonable connecting factor: The chosen governing-law jurisdiction is the same as, or has a substantive connection with, at least one of the place of contract performance, location of the subject matter, place where the services occur, place where the dispute arises, or place of incorporation of a signing party.
- Local-operations contract: A local contract signed by an overseas Protected Party entity for business conducted at its place of incorporation or actual operations, such as a local lease, local employment arrangement, or local procurement of services.

[Step 1: Identify whether a governing-law provision exists]
Scan the entire contract to determine whether it contains a provision expressly designating the laws of a particular country or region, or an international treaty, as the governing law.
- If yes, proceed to Step 3.
- If no, proceed to Step 2.

[Step 2: Assessment where no governing-law provision exists]
[Control Condition] If the contract meets any element of the definition of a foreign-related contract, flag a risk.
[Exemption] If the contract is not foreign-related, do not flag a risk.

[Step 3: Assessment where a governing-law provision exists]
[Control Conditions] Flag a risk if any of the following applies:
(1) A law or regulation cited in the provision is no longer in force, including but not limited to the "Civil Law of the People's Republic of China," "Contract Law of the People's Republic of China," "General Principles of Civil Law," or "Contract Law," as well as any other law whose name does not exist or that has been superseded by the Civil Code of the People's Republic of China;
(2) The Protected Party is a mainland China entity contracting with an overseas entity, and the chosen law is neither the law of mainland China (the Mainland) of the People's Republic of China nor the law of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region of the People's Republic of China, and the jurisdiction of the chosen law has no reasonable connecting factor with the contract.
[Exemptions] No risk is flagged if any of the following applies:
- The cited law or regulation is a currently effective law of the People's Republic of China;
- The contract provides for the application of the law of mainland China or the law of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region of the People's Republic of China;
- The Protected Party is an overseas Protected Party entity (including a Hong Kong, Macao, or Taiwan entity), signs a local-operations contract, and agrees to apply the local law of its place of incorporation/operations;
- The chosen law has a reasonable connecting factor with the contract, namely a substantive connection between the chosen jurisdiction and the place of performance, subject matter, services, dispute, or a party's place of incorporation;
- The contract is for the procurement of overseas professional services, such as overseas litigation/arbitration representation, local compliance consulting, or overseas applications or registrations for intellectual property, and provides for the law of the place where the services occur;
- The contract is an international financial/derivatives standard form, such as an ISDA Master Agreement and its annexes, whose governing law is the standard choice generally used for that form, such as English law or New York law;
- The contract is an agreement that does not dispose of substantive rights or interests, such as a free-trial agreement, confidentiality agreement/NDA, or cooperation memorandum; in that case, downgrade the matter to an advisory and do not treat it as a risk item.

[Scope] This rule reviews provisions on liability for breach in contracts. The Protected Party means the party served by the rule whose interests are to be protected; the Counterparty means the other party to the contract.

[Definitions]
- Provision on liability for breach: A provision specifying the liability and consequences to be borne by a contracting party that fails to perform or properly perform its contractual obligations, including liquidated damages, compensation for losses, payment of default interest, continued performance, and termination of the contract.
- Total liquidated damages/compensation: The fixed amount, cap on damages, or maximum amount calculated by a percentage that the Protected Party must pay for a particular breach under the provision.
- Material asymmetry: The liabilities of the Protected Party and the Counterparty for the same or a similar type of breach differ in any of the following ways: the liquidated-damages percentage or amount differs by a factor of two or more; liquidated damages/compensation liability is imposed on one party but not the other; or a liability cap is imposed on one party while the other has no cap.

[Step 1: Identify whether a provision on liability for breach exists]
- If none exists, do not flag a risk.
- If one exists, proceed to Step 2.

[Step 2: Assessment where a provision on liability for breach has been identified]
[Subject-Matter Limitation] All control conditions in this step apply only where the Protected Party is the breaching party, payment obligor, or liable party. If the provision binds only the Counterparty (the Counterparty is the breaching party/payment obligor), or is a unilateral provision that confers rights on or is favorable to the Protected Party, never trigger a risk.
[Control Conditions] Subject to the foregoing subject-matter limitation, flag a risk if any of the following applies:
(1) When the Protected Party is the breaching party, the cap on the total liquidated damages or compensation it bears meets any of the following thresholds:
    a. It exceeds 30% of the total contract value;
    b. The amount is RMB 200,000 or more;
    c. The daily liquidated-damages rate is greater than 0.05% (i.e., 0.5 per thousand per day).
(2) The provision ostensibly provides reciprocal liability for breach, but the cap on the total liquidated damages or compensation actually borne by the Protected Party still meets any threshold in Step 2(1);
(3) For the same breach, both liquidated damages and compensation for losses are provided, but the provision does not clarify the relationship between their application;
(4) The liabilities of the Protected Party and the Counterparty for breach are materially asymmetric, and the Protected Party bears the heavier liability.

[Exemptions]
(a) In item (3) above, if the provision expressly states that "where liquidated damages are insufficient to cover the losses, the breaching party shall compensate the shortfall," or contains an equivalent supplementary formulation (such as loss indemnity or making up the difference), the relationship between their application is deemed clarified and no risk is triggered;
(b) The provision binds only the Counterparty's breaches and provides only for the Counterparty's liquidated damages/compensation liability, while the Protected Party bears no corresponding liability; do not flag a risk;
(c) The provision is favorable because it confers rights on the Protected Party, such as the Protected Party's right to terminate unilaterally, claim liquidated damages, or withhold or offset payments; do not flag a risk;
(d) Any other circumstance that does not meet any of the foregoing control conditions.

[Scope] This rule reviews force-majeure arrangements in contracts. The Protected Party means the party served by the rule whose interests are to be protected; the Counterparty means the other party to the contract.

[Definitions]
- Force-majeure provision: A provision specifying the allocation of liability and the handling method where a contracting party is unable to perform its contractual obligations due to a force-majeure event.
- Existence of force-majeure content: The contract contains a dedicated force-majeure provision, or the term "force majeure" appears in another provision (such as exemption from liability, performance period, or termination) and its consequences or handling are addressed or referenced in any way.
- Contract under which the Protected Party charges fees: A contract under which the Protected Party provides services or delivers products and charges the Counterparty, such as a lease in which the Protected Party is the lessor, a service contract in which it is the service provider, or a sales or work contract in which it is the supplier/contractor. The determination is based on the flow of funds under the contract from the Counterparty to the Protected Party, and that flow must be supported by a specific payment/settlement provision; it must not be inferred from the contract type or the designations of Party A and Party B.
- Equivalent exemption mechanism for impediments to performance: A provision that, without using the term "force majeure," functionally provides for exemption from liability or allocation of risk for impediments to performance, including:
  a. In a technology development/R&D contract, a provision assigning the risk of failure or partial failure of research and development caused by technical difficulties that are difficult to overcome under the current level and conditions to one party or for separate negotiation by both parties;
  b. A provision for exemption from or limitation of liability where performance is impossible due to "objective reasons," "reasons not attributable to a party's fault," or "factors that cannot reasonably be foreseen, controlled, or overcome";
  c. A provision specifying how to handle, or allocating liability for, impediments to performance caused by specific events such as policy changes, regulatory requirements, epidemics, or natural disasters.
- Intra-group related-party transaction: All signing parties to the contract belong to the Protected Party's group, including its parent company, subsidiaries, branches, and affiliates under common ultimate control, with no transaction counterparty outside the group.
- Determinate fee amount: The total contract price expressly stated in the contract text that the Protected Party can determine it will receive. None of the following constitutes a determinate fee amount and must not be used to find that the threshold has been exceeded: a framework agreement or annual agreement without a fixed total price; actual-settlement or usage-based pricing without a minimum-spend commitment; a cap on performance-based incentives; an advance-payment or credit limit; a supply capacity or bandwidth cap; or expected future income.
- Preliminary document: A letter of intent, memorandum, preliminary agreement, or similar document that expressly makes the core commercial terms "subject to the formal contract." Its fee amount is the payment already determined under that document, such as an earnest-money payment.

[Step 1: Identify whether force-majeure content exists]
Scan the entire contract, including annexes, to determine whether any of the following applies:
(1) A dedicated force-majeure provision exists;
(2) The term "force majeure" appears in another provision, and its consequences or handling are addressed or referenced in any way;
(3) An equivalent exemption mechanism for impediments to performance, as defined in [Definitions], exists.
- If any of the foregoing exists, proceed to Step 2.
- If none exists, proceed to Step 3.

[Step 2: Assessment where force-majeure content exists]
[Exemption] Force majeure is deemed to have been addressed; do not flag a risk.

[Step 3: Assessment where no force-majeure content exists]
[Control Condition] Flag a risk only if both of the following are met (both must be supported by specific contract provisions and must not be inferred from the contract type, industry practice, or commercial speculation):
(1) The contract is one under which the Protected Party charges fees, meaning a specific payment/settlement provision demonstrates that funds flow from the Counterparty to the Protected Party;
(2) The determinate fee amount stated in the contract exceeds RMB 1 million.
[Exemptions] No risk is flagged if any of the following applies:
- The contract is not one under which the Protected Party charges fees, including where the Protected Party is the engaging party, purchaser, paying party, or borrower, or where funds flow out from the Protected Party;
- The contract is one under which the Protected Party charges fees, but the determinate fee amount is RMB 1 million or less;
- The contract does not state a determinate fee amount as defined in [Definitions], and the contract text cannot establish that total fees exceed the threshold;
- The contract is an intra-group related-party transaction as defined in [Definitions], with no external claim or commercial default exposure;
- The contract is a preliminary document as defined in [Definitions], and the amount already determined under it does not exceed the threshold;
- The contract substantively covers the consequences of impediments to performance through another mechanism, for example by providing that only amounts already received will be refunded if performance becomes impossible, limiting liability to a specified amount, or allocating the core risk to the Counterparty.

[Scope] This rule reviews the formal correctness of monetary amounts in contracts and the consistency between amounts written in uppercase Chinese financial numerals and lowercase numerals. The Protected Party means the party served by the rule whose interests are to be protected; the Counterparty means the other party to the contract.

[Definitions]
- Uppercase amount: An amount whose numeric portion is written in uppercase Chinese financial numerals. The numeric portion must contain at least one of "壹、贰、叁、肆、伍、陆、柒、捌、玖、拾、佰、仟." "零、万、亿、元、角、分、整" are units or supplementary characters and, on their own, are insufficient to constitute an uppercase amount.
- Exclusions from review: None of the following is an uppercase amount or falls within this rule, and no defect may be found on the basis of any of them:
  a. An amount expressed in Arabic numerals, such as "10500元," "￥282,974.60," "人民币10万元," or "30000.00元";
  b. An amount expressed in lowercase Chinese numerals, such as "三万元," "十五万元," or "五千元";
  c. A non-monetary number such as a date, reference number, sequence number, quantity, percentage, or duration, such as "2025年11月28日," "第2条," or "6%";
  d. A table heading or field unit, such as "Unit Price (Yuan)" or "Amount (RMB 10,000)."
- Conventional abbreviations: Forms consistent with customary Chinese monetary writing that have a unique meaning and leave no room for alteration do not constitute formal defects, including:
  a. Omission of the leading "壹," such as "拾万元整" or "拾伍万元," equivalent to "壹拾万元整" or "壹拾伍万元整";
  b. Use of "万元" or "亿元" as the monetary unit, such as "伍拾万元" or "壹拾亿元整";
  c. Use of "圆" as a legally recognized variant of "元," such as "壹拾陆万伍仟圆整";
  d. Direct connection of the jiao and fen digits where the yuan digit is zero, such as "壹拾陆万玖仟玖佰捌拾元贰角柒分";
  e. An amount precise to yuan or jiao without the appended character "整."
- Formatting marks: Typographic symbols or field annotations that are not part of the amount itself, including square brackets "【】," field-property annotations in parentheses such as "(uppercase)" or "(lowercase)," fill-in underscores, slash placeholders "/," blank markers such as "N/A" or "XX," and repeated unit wording immediately following an uppercase amount, such as the redundant "元整" in "【贰万元整】元整." These marks must be removed before assessing the formal correctness of the amount itself.
- Template placeholder amount: A monetary field that remains uncompleted, such as "人民币_N/A整," "人民币【】元," "(uppercase)____," or template sample values such as "1元/壹元," where the contract separately provides the basis for actual settlement. Such a field is not treated as a determined transaction amount and is excluded from review.
- Lowercase amount: An amount in Arabic numerals that refers to the same payment and the same context as a particular uppercase amount.
- Corresponding relationship: An uppercase amount and a lowercase amount are subject to comparison only when they refer to the same payment, such as the total contract price, the same liquidated damages, or the same security deposit within the same provision. The following do not constitute a corresponding relationship and must not be used to find an inconsistency where correspondence would otherwise be expected:
  a. A unilaterally stated amount, meaning liquidated damages, compensation, an assessment threshold, an account limit, a deposit principal, a security deposit, or a similar amount stated only in uppercase, with no matching lowercase field anywhere in the contract;
  b. Amounts for different payments, such as the total contract price versus liquidated damages, the tax-inclusive total versus the after-tax net amount, or a reference budget versus the actual contribution cap;
  c. A field in standard-form text that provides only an uppercase field, such as "Basic Deposit Limit (Uppercase)" in a banking agreement.
- Formal defect: After removing [Formatting Marks] and excluding [Conventional Abbreviations], an uppercase amount still fails any of the following requirements:
  a. Arabic numerals or lowercase Chinese numerals are mixed into the numeric portion, such as "十" in "伍十万元整" or "点" in "叁万壹仟捌佰叁拾柒点伍贰元整";
  b. No monetary unit ("元/万元/亿元") appears anywhere in the full text or in the same sentence or table row, so the unit of measurement cannot be determined;
  c. The Chinese characters are nonstandard, including typographical errors such as "签" in "陆签贰佰贰拾伍元" or "件" in "壹拾玖万伍件元整," non-statutory variant characters, repeated or stacked wording, or repeated units that create semantic confusion and prevent unique determination of the value.
- Consistency defect: An uppercase amount and a lowercase amount in a corresponding relationship have different values after conversion.

[Step 1: Identify whether any uppercase amount exists]
Scan the entire contract, first excluding [Exclusions from Review] and [Template Placeholder Amounts], then determine whether any expression meeting the definition of an uppercase amount appears, meaning that its numeric portion contains any of "壹贰叁肆伍陆柒捌玖拾佰仟."
- If no uppercase amount exists, do not flag a risk and end the review;
- If all monetary expressions in the contract fall within [Exclusions from Review] (Arabic numerals, lowercase Chinese numerals, dates or reference numbers, or table-heading units), do not flag a risk and end the review;
- If an uppercase amount exists, proceed to Step 2.

[Step 2: Assess formal correctness]
For each uppercase amount, first remove [Formatting Marks], then verify compliance with the formal requirements.
[Control Condition] If any uppercase amount has a formal defect, meaning any of circumstances a, b, or c in the definition, flag a risk and identify its location and type of defect.
[Exemptions] No risk is flagged if any of the following applies:
- All uppercase amounts use uppercase Chinese financial numerals, have a determinable monetary unit, and use standard characters;
- The wording falls within any of the [Conventional Abbreviations]: omission of the leading "壹," use of "万元/亿元" as the unit, use of "圆" for "元," direct connection of jiao and fen where the yuan digit is zero, or omission of "整";
- The defect arises solely from [Formatting Marks], such as square brackets, "(uppercase)" field annotations, underscore or slash placeholders, or redundant repeated units, and the amount itself is formally correct after those marks are removed;
- Although the monetary unit does not immediately follow the uppercase numerals, it is expressly stated in the main clause of the same sentence, such as "RMB 100,000 (uppercase: 壹拾万)," in an adjacent cell of the same table row, or in the field name;
- The amount is zero, such as "0万元整" or "(uppercase: 零) 0万元整," and has a unique, unambiguous meaning.

[Step 3: Assess consistency between uppercase and lowercase amounts]
For each uppercase amount, locate the lowercase amount referring to the same payment according to [Corresponding Relationship], convert the amounts, and compare them. Make the comparison only where both amounts exist; if no lowercase amount in a corresponding relationship is found, do not find a defect on that basis.
[Control Condition] If an uppercase amount and a lowercase amount in a corresponding relationship have different values after conversion, flag a risk and identify the location, the two values, and the magnitude of the discrepancy.
[Exemptions] No risk is flagged if any of the following applies:
- The contract contains no uppercase amount;
- All corresponding uppercase and lowercase amounts have exactly the same value after conversion;
- The uppercase amount falls within an exclusion under [Corresponding Relationship], such as a unilaterally stated amount, an amount for a different payment, or a standard-form field providing only an uppercase field, and there is no lowercase amount in the full text that should correspond to it;
- The uppercase and lowercase amounts are respectively tax-inclusive and tax-exclusive amounts, a total and an installment, or a cap and an actual payment, with an express conversion or subordination relationship explained in the contract;
- The numerical difference exists only between Arabic-numeral amounts, such as two inconsistent lowercase amounts or an error in thousands separators, and does not involve conversion and comparison between uppercase and lowercase; this falls outside the scope of this rule.

[Overall Conclusion] If all uppercase amounts pass Steps 2 and 3, do not flag a risk. If any amount has a formal defect or consistency defect, flag a risk and expressly identify whether it is a formal defect or a consistency defect.

[Scope] This rule reviews the completeness of bank account information in contracts. The Protected Party means the party served by the rule whose interests are to be protected; the Counterparty means the other party to the contract.

[Definitions]
- Three elements of bank account information: The following three items must all be stated for the same account:
  a. Account name: The name of the account holder;
  b. Name of the account-opening bank: The bank name, including the specific branch or sub-branch where the account is held;
  c. Account number: The digits of the bank account number.
- Bank account information involved: The contract actually provides specific content for at least one of the three elements. A general reference to a "bank account," "payment account," or "the account designated by the other party," without any specific element, does not mean bank account information is involved.
- Dynamic account reference: A provision directing the account elements to a medium outside the contract or a subsequent notice, such as "subject to the information displayed in the management portal," "subject to the account shown on the system page," "subject to the account notified by the other party in writing," "to be completed at the counter when a new account is opened," or "subject to the account stated in the settlement statement." Such wording is a mechanism for obtaining account information rather than an omission of an element; an element to which it refers is deemed not to be listed in the contract text.
- Determinable from the full text: An account element, although not listed together with the other elements, can be uniquely determined from the entire contract, for example:
  a. The name of the account-opening bank can be determined from wording such as "Party A opens an account with Party B" or "opens a settlement account with Party B," together with Party B's full bank name, including its branch, stated at the beginning of the contract;
  b. The account name can be determined from the signing-party information section, the heading of the signature/seal section, or the full party name at the beginning of the contract where the account information appears;
  c. Account elements are separated across the body, signature page, annexes, or different table cells, but all refer to the same account.
- Exclusions from the scope of review: The following information is excluded:
  a. A Fapiao information section, including the Fapiao title/name, taxpayer identification number, unified social credit code, and other tax identifiers, which is tax information used for Fapiao issuance and must not be confused with a funds receipt/payment account;
  b. Third-party payment platform accounts and other non-bank payment accounts, including accounts identified by an email address or mobile phone number, for which no "name of account-opening bank" element exists;
  c. Platform virtual account identifiers, such as master settlement accounts and settlement sub-accounts, provided by third-party payment/clearing institutions.
- Missing-element defect: After excluding elements covered by a [Dynamic Account Reference] and completing elements that are [Determinable from the Full Text], the information for the same account still contains only one or two of the three elements and lacks the remainder.

[Step 1: Identify whether bank account information is involved]
Scan the entire contract, first excluding information listed in [Exclusions from the Scope of Review] and elements covered by a [Dynamic Account Reference], then determine whether specific content for any of the three elements appears.
- If no specific element is identified, do not flag a risk and end the review;
- If all accounts involved in the contract fall within a type listed in [Exclusions from the Scope of Review], do not flag a risk and end the review;
- If at least one specific element is identified, proceed to Step 2.

[Step 2: Assess completeness of the three elements]
Verify the completeness of the three elements on a per-account basis rather than by text location. First consolidate elements dispersed across the body, signature page, annexes, tables, and different cells into the same account under [Determinable from the Full Text], then determine whether the account name, name of the account-opening bank, and account number can all be determined.
[Control Condition] If, after consolidation, an account still has only one or two of the three elements and a missing element cannot be determined from the entire contract, flag a risk and identify the missing element and its location.
[Exemptions] No risk is flagged if any of the following applies:
- The contract refers only generally to a "bank account" or similar concept and lists no specific element;
- All three elements are absent;
- All three elements of every account are stated, or a missing item can be completed under [Determinable from the Full Text];
- An element is not listed in the contract text because of a [Dynamic Account Reference], and the account information is dynamically provided through a medium outside the contract;
- The account falls within an [Exclusion from the Scope of Review], namely Fapiao/tax information, a third-party payment account, or a platform virtual settlement account;
- All three elements actually appear in the contract and only appear to be missing because of text-parsing issues such as misaligned table-field labels and content, splitting across cells or rows, or line-break truncation.

[Overall Conclusion] Flag a risk only where, after consolidating the full text by account, an account still lacks an element and the missing item cannot be determined. Do not flag a risk where no specific element is involved, an element can be determined from the full text, a dynamic account reference is provided, an exclusion applies, or only parsing noise exists.

[Scope] This rule reviews consistency among monetary expressions for which a currency is expressly indicated in a contract. The Protected Party means the party served by the rule whose interests are to be protected; the Counterparty means the other party to the contract.

[Definitions]
- Amount with an indicated currency: A specific amount accompanied, before or after the number, by a currency symbol or currency name, including but not limited to Renminbi, US dollar, euro, Hong Kong dollar, Japanese yen, and their corresponding symbols.
- Equivalent expressions of the same currency: Different written forms referring to the same legal tender are deemed consistent and do not constitute a conflict, including but not limited to:
  a. Renminbi: "人民币," "元," "万元," "亿元," "￥," "RMB," "CNY," and "圆";
  b. US dollar: "美元," "USD," and "US$";
  c. Hong Kong dollar: "港币," "港元," "HKD," and "HK$";
  d. The same correspondence applies to the names, ISO 4217 codes, and currency symbols of other currencies.
  In the context of commercial contracts within China, "元/万元" as legal units of measurement are inherently identical to "人民币/RMB/CNY," and no additional reconciliation provision is required.
- Comparable amounts: Only two or more amounts with indicated currencies that refer to the same payment constitute a comparison sample under this rule, including the same transaction consideration, pricing and settlement of the same payment, or different expressions of the same payable amount in different locations.
- Exclusions from the scope of review: The following do not constitute [Comparable Amounts] and are excluded from pairwise comparison:
  a. Amounts belonging to different legal relationships or contractual dimensions, such as transaction consideration versus a cap on liability, service fees versus confidentiality liquidated damages, service fees versus minimum insurance coverage, or the contract price versus a third-party transaction amount mentioned in the background facts;
  b. Amounts applicable in mutually exclusive scenarios, such as a fixed quotation versus an hourly rate for out-of-scope matters, or alternative amounts under different settlement methods;
  c. Parallel compliance thresholds connected by "or," such as "the working-meal allowance shall not exceed RMB 100 or USD 20," where the "or" logic itself provides a self-consistent rule of application;
  d. Non-specific monetary expressions, such as table headings or field units ("Unit Price (Yuan)," "Amount (RMB 10,000)"), uncompleted blank placeholders ("【】元," "Bank Account No. (HK$): -"), or the currency attribute of an account stated in an account-information section;
  e. A contract in which a currency is indicated in only one place.
- Currency reconciliation mechanism: A rule provided in the contract for handling different currency expressions, including:
  a. A priority provision stating that a particular currency shall prevail;
  b. A conversion provision such as "converted at the agreed exchange rate" or "compared after conversion into the contract currency";
  c. An agreed settlement currency or Fapiao issuance currency, such as "Fapiao shall be issued in Singapore dollars" or "the settlement currency shall be the currency agreed by both parties in writing at the time of settlement";
  d. An account or payment-path arrangement for payment in different currencies, such as "if payment is made in Renminbi or Macao pataca, the receiving account shall be subject to offline notice";
  e. A provision stating that a liability cap or similar amount shall be subject to the amount stated in another document, such as an engagement letter or order.
- Matters outside this rule: Errors in the numerical value itself or inconsistency between uppercase and lowercase, such as "15000万元" versus "壹万伍仟元整" or "198万" versus "198.01万," are issues of monetary accuracy and must be handled under the "Monetary amounts" rule; they must not be flagged as a risk in the name of "currency inconsistency" under this rule.

[Step 1: Extract comparable amounts with indicated currencies]
Search the entire contract and count all specific monetary expressions with expressly indicated currencies. Exclude item by item the circumstances listed in [Exclusions from the Scope of Review], then group amounts referring to the same payment according to the definition of [Comparable Amounts].
- If no group contains two or more comparable amounts, do not flag a risk and end the review;
- If two or more amounts refer to the same payment, proceed to Step 2.

[Step 2: Assess currency consistency]
Conduct pairwise comparisons only within the same [Comparable Amounts] group, and first normalize currencies under [Equivalent Expressions of the Same Currency]. Cross-payment and cross-legal-relationship comparisons are prohibited.
[Control Condition] If, within the same payment, two or more amounts still indicate different currencies after normalization and the difference is not addressed by a [Currency Reconciliation Mechanism], flag a risk and identify the conflicting locations, the respective currencies, and the provisions to which they belong.
[Exemptions] No risk is flagged if any of the following applies:
- The contract indicates no currency or indicates a currency in only one place;
- Two currency indications are [Equivalent Expressions of the Same Currency] referring to the same legal tender, such as "元" versus "人民币," "CNY" versus "人民币," or "￥" versus "人民币";
- All amounts with indicated currencies under the same payment are fully consistent;
- Amounts with different currencies fall within [Exclusions from the Scope of Review] and do not constitute a comparable sample;
- Different currency expressions are addressed by any form of [Currency Reconciliation Mechanism];
- The issue is actually a numerical typographical error or inconsistency between uppercase and lowercase and falls within the scope of "Monetary amounts";
- One of the amounts is a reference indication qualified by wording such as "approximately" or "equivalent to," for example "RMB 50,000 (approximately HKD 56,800)," and is not an independent pricing obligation.

[Overall Conclusion] This rule does not require the contract to expressly declare a currency. A risk is flagged only where, for the same payment, the currencies remain inconsistent after normalization and are not reconciled.

[Scope] This rule reviews the completeness and consistency of the signing parties' names at the beginning and end of a contract. The Protected Party means the party served by the rule whose interests are to be protected; the Counterparty means the other party to the contract.

[Definitions]
- Signing party: The Protected Party and the Counterparty to the contract; in a tripartite contract, the Protected Party, Counterparty A, and Counterparty B.
- Signing party name: The formally registered name of a signing party, limited to the following forms:
  a. The full name of an enterprise;
  b. The name of an individual;
  c. The full registered name of another organization.
- Valid means of identification: Identification of a party name is not limited to the format "Party X: name." Any of the following constitutes valid identification:
  a. The party-introduction section identifies the party in the form "Party A: name" or "Party B: name";
  b. The name appears in the introductory sentence of the first paragraph, such as "Party B, as engaged by Party A, shall..." or "This Agreement is entered into by A and B";
  c. The name appears in a table field, such as "Party A Fapiao Information—Entity Name," "Party A Account—Account Name," or "Institutional Customer—Name," "Provider/Customer/Venue Provider: Name";
  d. The name appears below the contract title, in a reference-number section, an information table, or a special-terms provision;
  e. The name appears on the signature page/in the execution section (used in Step 2).
- Ancillary Document: An order, execution order, confirmation, settlement statement, signature page, amendment, supplemental agreement, technical annex, price memorandum, or similar document whose text cites the name or number of the master agreement (framework agreement), or provides that "matters not covered herein shall be governed by the master agreement" or "together with the master contract, this document constitutes the complete legal document." The signing parties to such a document are deemed to have been fixed by the master agreement.
- Unilateral document: A document issued or completed by one party alone, such as a filing registration form, expense-allocation form, technical specification/technical requirements, quotation list, unilateral declaration, or undertaking, for which no beginning-and-end party structure exists in the sense of a bilateral contract.
- Text-extraction artifact: A textual discrepancy caused by PDF/OCR recognition, HTML table rendering, multi-column or multi-row layout, or similar factors, including line-break character separation, truncated or incomplete characters, garbled text, extra or missing spaces, differences between full-width and half-width brackets or punctuation such as "Co.,Ltd" versus "Co. Ltd.," missing characters caused by seal obstruction, or cell content being split or merged.
- Substantive name discrepancy: Only the following four categories constitute inconsistent signing-party names:
  a. The trade name (core identifying wording) is changed, added, or omitted, such as "XX (China) Software Co., Ltd." versus "XX (China) Co., Ltd.";
  b. An administrative-division prefix is added or omitted, such as "a business in Longhua District, Haikou" versus "a business in Longhua District, Haikou City";
  c. An abbreviation, brand name, or trade name is used instead of the full name, such as only a brand name at the beginning but the full legal name at the end;
  d. The beginning and end refer to different legal entities, meaning a different company is substituted.
- Exclusions from the scope of review: The following information is associated with a party name but is not reviewed under this rule:
  a. Addresses;
  b. Contact persons and contact details;
  c. Signature/seal specimens;
  d. Execution dates;
  e. Lists of annexes;
  f. Non-registered-name fields such as brand names, trademarks, project names, and product names;
  g. Either language version of a bilingual Chinese-English name. Use of the Chinese full name at one end and the English full name at the other is not considered inconsistent where both refer to the same entity.

[Step 0: Preliminary applicability assessment]
- If the contract is a unilateral document as defined in [Definitions], this rule does not apply; do not flag a risk and end the review;
- If the contract is an Ancillary Document as defined in [Definitions], and it has cited the master agreement name or number or established the parties' identities through designations such as Party A and Party B, the signing parties are deemed identified; skip the omission assessment in Step 1 and proceed to Step 3 only if a name appears at the end;
- If the contract text is empty, no body text has been extracted, or a party-name field is an uncompleted template placeholder such as "【】" or "Party B:" followed by a blank, or an underscore, this is missing data or an item awaiting completion rather than a substantive violation; do not flag a risk.

[Step 1: Locate and identify the signing-party name identifications]
Within the beginning of the contract and the full text, locate the identification of each signing party's name according to valid means of identification a through d in [Definitions], and determine whether every signing party's name has been identified. A name must not be deemed missing merely because the format "Party X: name" is not used or no standalone party-introduction section exists.

[Step 2: Locate and identify signing-party names at the end of the contract]
Locate the identification of each signing party's name in the execution/signature section at the end of the contract, and determine whether each signing party's name appears.

[Step 3: Compare party names at the beginning and end]
For each signing party whose name appears at both the beginning and the end, first normalize [Text-Extraction Artifacts] as defined in [Definitions] by removing extra spaces and line breaks, standardizing full-width and half-width brackets and punctuation, and restoring split or merged cell content. Then determine whether the two names constitute a [Substantive Name Discrepancy] as defined in [Definitions].

[Control Conditions] Flag a risk if either of the following applies:
(1) In Step 1, the contract lacks the name identification of any signing party, meaning that party's registered name does not appear anywhere in the full text through any valid means of identification; flag a risk.
(2) In Step 3, the name of the same signing party at the beginning and end constitutes a [Substantive Name Discrepancy], limited to the four categories of a changed trade name, an added or omitted administrative-division prefix, substitution of an abbreviation for the full name, or reference to a different legal entity; flag a risk and identify the type of discrepancy.

[Exemptions] No risk is flagged if any of the following applies:
- Every signing party's name has been identified, and the corresponding name at the end has no substantive discrepancy from the name at the beginning;
- Every signing party's name has been identified, but the corresponding name does not appear at the end, or the end contains only a referential label such as "Party A (Seal)" or "Institutional Customer (Official Seal)" without repeating the full name (only the end is missing);
- The beginning-end discrepancy is solely a [Text-Extraction Artifact], and the names are identical after normalization;
- The beginning and end respectively use the Chinese full name and English full name of the same entity, or the end uses the Chinese full name from a bilingual Chinese-English name stated at the beginning (this is a subset of the name, not mixed use of an abbreviation);
- The contract is an Ancillary Document or unilateral document and has been exempted in Step 0;
- The discrepancy concerns the Counterparty's name and is an extraction artifact or non-substantive discrepancy that does not affect the determination of the Protected Party's rights and obligations.

[Scope] This rule reviews the continuity, uniqueness, and regularity of numbering at all levels in contracts. The Protected Party means the party served by the rule whose interests are to be protected; the Counterparty means the other party to the contract.

[Definitions]
- Hierarchical number: Any hierarchical number within a numbering system in the contract, including but not limited to:
  a. Numbers of chapters, articles, paragraphs, and items;
  b. Annex numbers;
  c. Schedule numbers;
  d. Figure and table numbers.
- Numbering form: Includes but is not limited to the following forms:
  a. "One/Two/Three...";
  b. "1/2/3...";
  c. "(1)/(2)/(3)...";
  d. "1.1/1.2/1.3...";
  e. "A/B/C...."
- Same-level group: A set of numbers delimited by the "immediate parent provision." Only numbers under the same immediate parent and at the same numbering level form one group, for example:
  a. All "Article X" numbers under the same chapter form one group;
  b. All "(X)" numbers under the same parent provision form one group; "(X)" numbers under different parent provisions belong to different groups and are not compared for continuity across groups;
  c. Annexes, schedules, figures, and tables are each grouped independently according to their respective documents.
- Lawful hierarchical reset: Numbering restarts from the initial value, such as 1, (1), or One, upon entering a new parent provision. This is normal numbering and does not constitute a skipped or missing number.

[Step 1: Group numbers by hierarchy]
Group all numbers in the contract according to their respective levels.

[Step 2: List numbers in order of appearance within each group]
Within the same group, list the numbers in the order in which they appear in the contract.

[Step 3: Examine the number sequence in each group]
Examine the sequence in each group against all of the following requirements:
(1) Whether the numbers are consecutive according to the established numbering rule, such as ascending numbers or letters, with no skipped numbers between adjacent entries.
(2) Whether the same group contains no duplicate number.
(3) Whether the group contains no number that should appear but has been omitted, such as "Article One, Article Three" with "Article Two" missing.

[Control Conditions] Flag a risk if any of the following applies (every determination is limited to the same group delimited by the immediate parent provision, and cross-group comparison is prohibited):
(1) A number in a same-level group is skipped or missing; flag a risk.
(2) A duplicate number appears within a same-level group; flag a risk.
(3) Incompatible numbering rules are mixed within a same-level group, making it impossible to determine whether the sequence is consecutive; flag a risk.

[Exemptions] No risk is flagged if any of the following applies:
- The numbers in every hierarchical group in the contract are consecutive, with no duplicate or missing numbers;
- The numbering is a lawful hierarchical reset, meaning numbering restarts under a new parent provision;
- The same number, such as "(1)," appears under different parent provisions in multiple places; because these belong to different groups, this is not treated as duplication;
- Only global numbering across groups or chapters is discontinuous; this is not used as a basis for finding a skipped or missing number.

[Scope] This rule reviews the textual accuracy of an entire contract and identifies typographical errors, spelling errors, and nonstandard character usage. The Protected Party means the party served by the rule whose interests are to be protected; the Counterparty means the other party to the contract.

[Definitions]
- Typographical error: A character usage error in the contract text that is inconsistent with standard character usage or spelling, specifically including the following types:
  a. Misuse of a visually similar Chinese character: A Chinese character whose shape resembles the intended character but whose meaning does not match;
  b. Misuse of a phonetically similar Chinese character: A Chinese character with the same or a similar pronunciation as the intended character, including homophones and near-homophones;
  c. Omission of a Chinese character: A necessary Chinese character is missing from a word or fixed expression, making the word incomplete;
  d. Repetition of a Chinese character: An unnecessary repeated Chinese character appears in a word or fixed expression;
  e. Incorrect Chinese word combination: A combination of near-synonymous characters/words that does not fit the context and constitutes nonstandard usage;
  f. English spelling error: Letters in a word are transposed, repeated, omitted, or incorrectly used, making the spelling inconsistent with a standard dictionary;
  g. Incorrect mixing of punctuation or numbers with text: Such mixing causes the meaning to deviate from standard expression.

[Step 1: Proofread the entire contract character by character]
Scan the entire contract and check item by item whether any of the foregoing types of typographical error exists.
- If a suspected erroneous character is found, proceed to Step 2.
- If no suspected error is found anywhere in the contract, do not flag a risk.

[Step 2: Assess suspected erroneous characters]
[Control Conditions] If any of the following applies, flag a risk and output the specific location of the typographical error and a suggested correction:
(1) Misuse of a visually similar Chinese character;
(2) Misuse of a phonetically similar Chinese character, including homophones and near-homophones;
(3) Omission of a Chinese character, making a word incomplete;
(4) Repetition of a Chinese character, creating an unnecessary repeated character;
(5) Incorrect Chinese word combination, resulting in nonstandard usage in context;
(6) English spelling error, including transposition, repetition, omission, or incorrect use of letters;
(7) Incorrect mixing of punctuation or numbers with text, where the substantive-harm threshold has been met, meaning the mixing causes a deviation or ambiguity in the meaning of key rights and obligations, amounts, time limits, or scope of liability.
[Substantive-Harm Threshold] For item (7), issue an alert only where the mixing causes a deviation or ambiguity in the meaning of key rights and obligations, amounts, time limits, or liability. Typographic mixing that does not affect comprehension, such as mixed full-width and half-width punctuation, inconsistent punctuation after numbering, or spaces between numbers and units, is not flagged as a risk; an editorial advisory may be given where necessary.
[Exemptions]
- Formatting marks in sample text or standard-form templates, such as checkboxes (□/☑), fill-in underscores, placeholders such as "__" or "XX," and table separators, are not treated as incorrect mixing of punctuation or numbers with text; do not flag a risk;
- A proper noun in the contract, such as a personal name, place name, enterprise name, product name, trademark, or technical term, is consistent with its officially registered or generally accepted standard spelling; do not flag a risk;
- A coined term, abbreviation, or designation is expressly defined by the parties and used consistently in the contract; do not flag a risk;
- The original wording of a law, regulation, or standard document cited in the contract is consistent with the cited document; do not flag a risk.

[Scope] This rule reviews the fairness of assignment provisions in contracts and the protection of the Protected Party's interests. The Protected Party means the party served by the rule whose interests are to be protected; the Counterparty means the other party to the contract.

[Definitions]
- Assignment provision: A provision under which a contracting party assigns all of its rights and obligations under the contract, or its status as a contracting party, to a third party, resulting in a change of contracting party.
- Exclusions from review: The following acts do not constitute an assignment of contractual rights and obligations or contractual status in their entirety and are excluded from this rule. When identified, they must not be used as a basis for finding asymmetry:
  a. Further disposition of the right to use a leased item, venue, equipment, duct, or other subject matter, namely subleasing, sub-renting, lending, or permitting use by a third party;
  b. Sublicensing/re-licensing of intellectual property, or restrictions on the scope of a brand or materials license;
  c. Delegation and division of performance, namely sub-delegation, transfer of contracted work, subcontracting, designation of an affiliate or cooperation partner to perform on a party's behalf, or engagement of an internal branch to handle matters;
  d. A standalone assignment of a receivable, such as an account receivable, collection right, or negotiated payment, or factoring or pledging of an account receivable;
  e. Disposition of real rights in the subject matter itself, such as an owner selling or mortgaging its property or assets;
  f. Assignment of, or restrictions on pledging, rights and interests in a financial product, such as a prohibition on assigning or pledging the principal and returns of a structured deposit;
  g. Expansion of or addition to the scope of contracting parties, such as permitting a party to add an affiliate as a co-signing party.
- Assignment to an affiliate: An assignment to a company directly or indirectly controlled by, controlling, or under common control with the assignor.
- General authorization for affiliate assignments: The contract provides through any of the following that the Protected Party may assign rights and obligations to an affiliate without obtaining the Counterparty's further consent, and is therefore deemed to satisfy the exemption for assignments to affiliates:
  a. A definition provision in the contract has included the Protected Party's affiliates within the scope of that party, the permitted assignees, or "permitted assignees";
  b. The provision expressly states that "internal group adjustments/internal reorganizations are not subject to the restrictions of this Article";
  c. A separate annex, confidentiality agreement, or special provision grants the Protected Party independent discretion to transfer information or rights to designated affiliates;
  d. The contract has directly listed the Protected Party's affiliates as authorized parties or rights holders.
- Expressly broader assignment right: The contract expressly grants the Counterparty a broader assignment right than the Protected Party, for example by providing that the Counterparty's assignment requires "only prior written notice," "no consent from the other party," "free assignment to any third party," or "assignment to its affiliates without consent." Merely making no provision regarding the Counterparty's assignment does not constitute an expressly broader assignment right. Under Articles 551 and 555 of the Civil Code, absent such a provision, the Counterparty's assignment of obligations likewise requires the Protected Party's consent, and the parties are therefore substantively equal at law.
- Statutory/regulatory assignment restriction: A restriction necessarily arising from a law, administrative regulation, regulatory requirement, or the nature of the subject matter, rather than unequal bargaining power, including:
  a. The Counterparty is a government authority, administrative entity, or state-owned asset administrator, and its contractual rights and obligations cannot be freely assigned under the principle that administrative powers and duties are statutory;
  b. Regulatory requirements applicable to licensed financial institutions, real-name account systems, anti-money-laundering rules, or prohibitions on unlawful transfers of deposits;
  c. The subject matter is personal in nature or based on a high degree of trust, such as research and development, ride-hailing driving services, livestreaming, licensing of a performer's likeness, or legal services, making a change of performing party inappropriate;
  d. A compliance requirement in a government procurement or tendering project prohibiting the successful bidder from changing the performing party without authorization.
- Assessment principle: This rule does not use "whether the Protected Party's assignment is prohibited" as the sole criterion. A risk arises only where express contractual wording creates an asymmetric arrangement that is stricter for the Protected Party and more permissive for the Counterparty. Reciprocal restrictive provisions, a statutorily equal position, and provisions restricting only the Counterparty do not constitute risks.

[Step 1: Identify whether an assignment provision exists in the contract]
Scan the entire contract, first excluding acts listed in [Exclusions from Review], then determine whether it contains an arrangement concerning the assignment of contractual rights and obligations or contractual status in their entirety. In making the determination, quote the original provision and explain whether its regulated object is a "change of contractual status" rather than use of the subject matter, sublicensing of intellectual property, subcontracting of performance, or a standalone assignment of a receivable.
- If one exists, proceed to Step 2;
- If none exists, or every provision containing the word "assignment" falls within [Exclusions from Review], do not flag a risk.

[Step 2: Assess whether the assignment provision is fair or favorable to the Protected Party]
[Control Conditions] Flag a risk if any of the following applies (each item requires an [Expressly Broader Assignment Right] in the contract as a necessary condition, and the original provisions restricting the Protected Party and relaxing the Counterparty must both be quoted):
(1) The provision prohibits or restricts the Protected Party's assignment of contractual rights and obligations while expressly granting the Counterparty an [Expressly Broader Assignment Right];
(2) The provision expressly permits the Counterparty to assign contractual rights and obligations without obtaining the Protected Party's written consent, or with notice only, while imposing a stricter restriction such as written consent on the Protected Party's assignment;
(3) The provision requires the Protected Party's assignment to obtain the Counterparty's written consent while expressly providing that the Counterparty's assignment does not require the Protected Party's consent;
(4) The provision restricts the Protected Party's assignment to its affiliate by requiring the Counterparty's consent while expressly providing that the Counterparty's assignment to its affiliate does not require the Protected Party's consent.
[Exemptions] No risk is flagged if any of the following applies:
- The contract contains a [General Authorization for Affiliate Assignments], and the Protected Party's assignment to its affiliate does not require the Counterparty's further consent;
- The provision makes reciprocal arrangements for the assignment rights and restrictions of the Protected Party and the Counterparty, meaning both parties' assignments require the other party's written consent, neither party's assignment requires the other party's consent, or both parties' assignments to affiliates do not require the other party's consent;
- The provision restricts only the Counterparty's assignment, while the Protected Party's assignment is unrestricted or subject to a more permissive restriction;
- The contract restricts only the Protected Party's assignment and makes no provision regarding the Counterparty's assignment, so no [Expressly Broader Assignment Right] is created. Under Articles 551 and 555 of the Civil Code, the parties are substantively equal and no risk is triggered;
- The restriction on the Protected Party is a [Statutory/Regulatory Assignment Restriction], such as where the Counterparty is a government or state-owned entity, a financial regulatory compliance requirement applies, the subject matter is personal in nature, or a government procurement or tendering compliance requirement applies, rather than being caused by unequal bargaining power;
- The Counterparty objectively has no legal room or commercial basis for assigning its contractual status under the contract, such as an administrative authority or the party bearing statutory obligations as a licensed financial institution, and the absence of a reciprocal restriction results from the nature of that entity;
- The Counterparty's assignment right is subject to substantive conditions, such as being limited to an internal reorganization of its global network, limited to transfer with the disposition of assets to a new owner, or still requiring execution of a supplemental agreement or confirmation by the Protected Party; or although the Protected Party's assignment requires consent, the provision states that the Counterparty "shall not unreasonably withhold consent," so no substantive asymmetry arises.

[Scope] This rule reviews the completeness of language provisions in multilingual contracts. The Protected Party means the party served by the rule whose interests are to be protected; the Counterparty means the other party to the contract.

[Definitions]
- Language provision: A provision expressly specifying the formal language (working language/official language) of the contract, and the language version that prevails in the event of ambiguity or conflict between multilingual texts. The identification criterion is met if either of the following characteristics applies:
  a. The provision expressly designates one language as the formal/official/working language of the contract;
  b. The provision expressly specifies which language version prevails in the event of ambiguity, conflict, or inconsistency between different language texts.
- Multilingual contract: A contract in which the same content is fully written in two or more natural languages, and each language version has legal effect, or no version is stated to prevail. A typical form is a Chinese-English bilingual provision-by-provision presentation in which both versions form part of the contract provisions themselves.
- Circumstances that are not multilingual contracts: The following do not constitute a multilingual contract and do not proceed to Step 2:
  a. In a standardized document such as a purchase order (PO), price memorandum, settlement statement, or financial notice, the foreign language merely provides label translations for Chinese fields or internationally accepted commercial terms such as Incoterms, Payment Terms, or Quantity, and the core commercial content is expressed in only one language or in pure numerical form;
  b. The contract as a whole is written in one language, with only a small number of foreign-language proper nouns, terms, abbreviations, entity names, or official patent or product names;
  c. Foreign-language content in an annex is a separately executed legal document, with independent signing parties, date, and signature fields, governing a different legal relationship rather than serving as a translation of the master contract provisions;
  d. A language version contains obvious spelling errors or incomplete content, or is merely an annotation/informative translation, and objectively does not have the same legal effect as the other version;
  e. The contract structure, definitions, and core rights-and-obligations provisions are all written in one language, while the other language appears only in business data, operating instructions, or name references in an annex.
- Equivalent interpretation benchmark: Although no dedicated language provision exists, the contract has established a unique interpretation benchmark through another arrangement, including:
  a. It specifies the language of arbitration or litigation proceedings;
  b. Both the agreed dispute-resolution venue and governing law point to the same jurisdiction, and that jurisdiction's official language is the same as one of the texts;
  c. It provides that "in the event of conflict with the master agreement/framework agreement, the master agreement shall prevail," and the master agreement is executed in a single language or already contains a language provision;
  d. It expressly provides that a particular language version takes effect or is executed.
- Substantive textual conflict: The two language versions express core content, such as the scope of rights and obligations, amounts, time limits, or allocation of liability, inconsistently enough to produce different interpretive conclusions. Differences only in wording style, word order, or translation convention are excluded.

[Step 1: Identify the languages used in the contract text]
Scan the body and annexes of the contract, first excluding all circumstances listed in [Circumstances That Are Not Multilingual Contracts], then determine whether it constitutes a multilingual contract as defined in [Definitions].
- If it constitutes a multilingual contract, meaning the same content is fully presented in two languages and each version has legal effect, proceed to Step 2;
- If any circumstance in [Circumstances That Are Not Multilingual Contracts] applies, or only one language is used, proceed to Step 3.

[Step 2: Assessment for a multilingual contract]
[Control Conditions] Flag a risk if either of the following applies:
(1) The contract constitutes a multilingual contract, but no language provision is identified, meaning neither a formal/official/working language nor a rule for precedence in the event of textual conflict is specified, and no [Equivalent Interpretation Benchmark] exists; flag a risk;
(2) A [Substantive Textual Conflict] already exists between the two language versions, meaning inconsistent expressions of rights and obligations, amounts, time limits, or liability; flag a high risk and identify the conflicting provision and the differences between the two versions. This item is not exempt merely because a language provision exists, but may be downgraded to an advisory where a clear prevailing text has been specified.
[Exemptions] No risk is flagged if any of the following applies:
- The contract already contains a language provision meeting either identification criterion in the definition;
- The contract already has an [Equivalent Interpretation Benchmark], such as an agreed arbitration/litigation language, a dispute-resolution venue and governing law pointing to the same jurisdiction, an agreed prevailing master agreement that already contains a language provision, or an expressly effective language version;
- The contract is an ancillary document such as an annex, order, or memorandum, and its master agreement already contains a language provision or provides that the framework agreement executed in Chinese shall prevail;
- The core provisions of the two language versions consist of purely numerical or coded fields, such as a price list, material number, or licensed vehicle model, with completely corresponding Chinese and foreign-language labels and no room for ambiguity;
- The contract falls within [Circumstances That Are Not Multilingual Contracts] and should have been excluded in Step 1.

[Step 3: Assessment for a single-language contract]
[Exemptions]
- The contract is written in only one language; do not flag a risk;
- Although the contract contains a small number of foreign-language words such as proper nouns, terms, or abbreviations, the complete contract text as a whole is written in a single language; do not flag a risk.

[Scope] This rule reviews the existence of a prohibition on external publication and the mandatory inclusion of such a provision in specified scenarios. The Protected Party means the party served by the rule whose interests are to be protected; the Counterparty means the other party to the contract.

[Definitions]
- Prohibition on external publication: A provision under which one or all parties must not publish, publicize, disclose, or release the existence of the contract, its content, the fact of cooperation, or related specified information to the public, media, or unauthorized third parties. Its core purpose is to control external dissemination of information. The identification criterion is met if any of the following characteristics applies:
  a. The provision expressly prohibits external disclosure, publication, or publicity concerning the existence or content of the contract without the other party's written consent;
  b. The provision expressly prohibits press releases, media coverage, marketing publicity, or public statements concerning the fact of cooperation without the other party's written consent;
  c. A confidentiality obligation covers external publication of information such as "the fact of contract execution, the cooperative relationship, or the contract content."
- External market-based investment or financing transaction: A transaction in which the Protected Party or its group establishes an equity or debt relationship with a third party outside the group, meaning a master transaction agreement under which funds or equity move between the group and an external entity.
- Intra-group funding arrangement: A loan, fund transfer, cash pooling, shareholder loan, internal credit-limit allocation, or execution document under a group's unified credit framework among parties all belonging to the Protected Party's group, including its parent company, subsidiaries, branches, and affiliates under common ultimate control. Such a contract involves no external information-disclosure scenario or public-opinion risk and is not a major investment or financing agreement.
- Materiality threshold: The "material" requirement is met only where the investment or financing amount, including equity investment, debt principal, or financing facility, reaches RMB 10 million or more, or the equivalent in foreign currency. An investment or financing arrangement below this threshold is not a scenario requiring mandatory inclusion of the provision.
- Ancillary professional services agreement: An agreement providing ancillary professional services for an investment, financing, or merger and acquisition transaction, such as a legal services engagement letter, lawyer engagement agreement, due-diligence services agreement, financial advisory agreement, or audit or valuation services agreement. Its subject matter is the provision of services rather than the financing rights and obligations themselves, so it is not an investment/financing agreement or merger and acquisition transaction agreement. The mandatory inclusion requirement does not apply even if its background involves a major transaction.
- Substantive strategic cooperation agreement: A top-level agreement that is framework-based and long-term, has external declaratory effect, and provides for the overall cooperation direction and resource-input mechanism. The following are not substantive strategic cooperation agreements:
  a. A specific implementation agreement, annual agreement, order, or confirmation under a master agreement or framework agreement;
  b. A specific implementation contract for a single event, individual project, or single promotional campaign;
  c. A document of intent expressly stating that it "serves only as the basis for cooperation intentions" or that "specific matters shall be separately set out in a formal contract";
  d. A contract whose subject matter is specific procurement, services, licensing, or allocation of R&D funding, even if its title contains "cooperation agreement."
- Publicity-purpose contract: A contract whose purpose itself includes external publication, joint publicity, or release of results, such as a joint promotional agreement, sponsorship or naming-rights agreement, voucher or marketing campaign cooperation agreement, public-welfare project cooperation agreement, industry-education integration or joint-development agreement, or IP licensing and joint promotion agreement. Where its provisions expressly grant publicity or display rights, joint release of reports, media coverage, or exhibition promotion, mandatory inclusion of a prohibition on external publication would conflict with the contract's purpose.
- Criteria for identifying "Specified Entity Wording": The signing party's registered corporate name must contain the wording in full; an oral abbreviation or use of a trademark is not sufficient.
- Mandatory-inclusion scenario: The contract nature meets both the substantive and threshold requirements and falls within any of the following:
  a. The contract is the master transaction agreement for an [External Market-Based Investment or Financing Transaction], including equity investment, debt investment, or a financing arrangement, and meets the [Materiality Threshold];
  b. The contract is a merger and acquisition transaction agreement, or an agreement providing for merger and acquisition due-diligence arrangements themselves, excluding an [Ancillary Professional Services Agreement] providing services for such a transaction;
  c. The contract is a [Substantive Strategic Cooperation Agreement], and the company name of any signing party contains in full the preset "Specified Entity Wording."

[Step 1: Identify whether a prohibition on external publication exists in the contract]
Scan the entire contract to determine whether it contains a prohibition on external publication meeting the foregoing definition.
- If one exists, do not flag a risk.
- If none exists, proceed to Step 2.

[Step 2: Determine whether the contract falls within a mandatory-inclusion scenario]
[Control Condition] If any of the following applies and no prohibition on external publication is identified, flag a risk and recommend adding the provision. The determination must quote contract provisions to explain whether the transaction counterparty is outside the group, whether the amount meets the threshold, and whether the agreement is the master transaction agreement:
(1) The contract is the master transaction agreement for an [External Market-Based Investment or Financing Transaction], and the investment or financing amount meets the [Materiality Threshold] of RMB 10 million or more, or the equivalent in foreign currency;
(2) The contract is a merger and acquisition transaction agreement, or an agreement providing for merger and acquisition due-diligence arrangements themselves;
(3) The contract is a [Substantive Strategic Cooperation Agreement], and the registered corporate name of any signing party contains the preset "Specified Entity Wording" in full.
[Exemptions] No risk is flagged if any of the following applies:
- The contract falls within none of the three mandatory-inclusion scenarios above;
- The contract is an [Intra-Group Funding Arrangement], such as a loan, fund transfer, cash pooling, shareholder loan, intra-group credit, or an execution document under a group's unified credit framework among affiliates, with no external disclosure scenario;
- The investment or financing amount does not meet the [Materiality Threshold] and therefore does not satisfy the "material" requirement;
- The contract is an [Ancillary Professional Services Agreement], such as a legal services engagement letter, due-diligence services, financial advisory services, or audit or valuation services, whose subject matter is services rather than financing itself;
- Although the contract contains "cooperation agreement" in its title, it is not a [Substantive Strategic Cooperation Agreement], but rather a specific implementation agreement, single-event agreement, annual project agreement, document of intent, or specific procurement or licensing contract; a risk must not be triggered merely because a signing party's name matches;
- The contract is a [Publicity-Purpose Contract] whose provisions expressly provide for publicity or display rights, joint publication, or media promotion, and mandatory inclusion of the provision would conflict with the contract's purpose;
- A confidentiality provision already substantively covers restrictions on external disclosure of "the fact of contract execution, the cooperative relationship, or the contract content," or the Protected Party's group already has a unified external-disclosure approval system governing such information.
